WEBVTT

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:25.243
So, hi, my name is Marcus, and first I want to apologize to everybody who attended the event. Normally I'm recording them, but last time the quality was very, very bad. And when I was editing the podcast, I decided to just record it anew because it was unbearable to hear in the recording.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:48.029
And my suspicion was that it was also unbearable during the presentation, so I'm very thankful for all the people who stuck around to learn something. It was under really difficult conditions, so I really appreciate that and I want to say thank you. So I want to repeat the event in the best way that I can.

00:00:48.737 --> 00:01:06.348
And yeah, so here it is. The topic of the event is A History of Consciousness. And this is all based on the views of Carl Gustav Jung on consciousness and human beings and how consciousness developed in human beings.

00:01:07.504 --> 00:01:23.822
Carl Gustav Jung was a Swiss psychologist, lived from 1875 to 1961, and was an important person for the beginning of psychology, which was back then bleeding edge, completely new.

00:01:24.352 --> 00:01:39.670
Jung often talked about how it's the youngest science there is, and he had his reasons for that, to think about it this way. And when we talk about the history of consciousness, we should first start talking about what consciousness is.

00:01:40.826 --> 00:01:58.871
And for Jung, consciousness was the ability to discriminate to make a separation. For him, the unconscious was something very dark. Dark in the sense that you cannot really see and you can't differentiate.

00:01:59.256 --> 00:02:14.917
For example, when you're in a room and it has no light, it can even be a room that you're familiar with, but you don't really know where one thing begins and the other thing ends. You're not sure what something is. We are extremely visual creatures.

00:02:15.622 --> 00:02:34.497
A big part of our brain is for visual information processing, which is unusual because for most animals it's smell, but for human beings it's sight. And when it's something unconscious, you cannot really grasp what it is and you have to gather more information what something is.

00:02:35.394 --> 00:02:57.759
And so when you shine a light in the room, then you can see the things, you can discriminate them and say, okay, this is a bed, this is a cupboard, this is a pillow. Before, you were really just guessing. And it's important to discriminate things, to separate things. And this is one of the functions of consciousness.

00:02:57.952 --> 00:03:22.497
And for Jung, it was very important that consciousness can fulfill this function to really dissect the problem. Another example, you have a car. You drive down the road and you realize suddenly something is happening and your car's not driving anymore. So something very simple, your car, at least in your perception, as something that drives suddenly is not driving anymore.

00:03:22.561 --> 00:03:45.681
And you have to find out what the problem is. So you start to investigate and all the stuff you have not thought about before suddenly comes into your consciousness because you're applying your attention to it. And you are separating different parts. You're checking, is a gas full? Is a battery full? Is something wrong with oil? And so on.

00:03:45.745 --> 00:04:09.157
You start checking all these things that you have before have not thought about. Car was a car and only by investigating it consciously, it splits up in all those different parts that all have a different function and are another all different sources of possible failure. So this is consciousness, a willful act.

00:04:10.424 --> 00:04:30.049
And your consciousness consists of everything that you know and everything you can do, everything you have acquired as knowledge, as skills over your lifetime. So memory is also very associated for Jung with consciousness. And it is discrimination also an action to do things or not to do things.

00:04:31.694 --> 00:04:44.205
To have a complex action as fixing a car and saying, okay, I will not check the paint, right? I will not check the glove compartment to see if tissues are there.

00:04:46.069 --> 00:05:08.398
To not do a lot of things, that's also a sign of consciousness for Jung, to self-regulate one's actions and to keep on those actions. And this is something that, let's say, smaller children have a problem with, to just keep attention at something. The attention shifts.

00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:21.461
So only when people get older and older, they gain the ability to really stick to plans, to formulate a plan, which is already a conscious action, and sticking to them, which is also a conscious action.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:47.118
While this all sounds very, very great for all the reasons that we could think of, as he says, modern human beings are very conscious in their ways and very sophisticated in their consciousness. As we've built up all the institutions, all the education to foster consciousness with all its abilities.

00:05:49.202 --> 00:06:11.825
What he saw is that you become blindsided in the way that when you focus on something, you block out everything else. It's a little bit like the eye. When you have your eye and you have the retina, there's only a small spot that can really produce high-resolution images.

00:06:12.988 --> 00:06:33.099
But you don't think about it that way. You think everything around you is high-resolution. But you don't really see it. It's a trick of the mind. When you stick out your arm and look at your thumb, the thumbnail is roughly the size of this spot on the retina that is high resolution. So it's really just a small dot.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:54.901
And what your brain automatically does is collecting all those high-resolution information and giving you the impression that everything you see is very high resolution. But the brain, when it's focused on something, blocks out everything else. It discriminates. It picks up a very specific part that you are, were looking for in reality and blocks out everything else.

00:06:55.591 --> 00:07:12.006
The same as a surgeon concentrates only on a very small part on the whole body. The whole body is huge, it's complex, just biologically wise, but it's also a person with a complex life and emotional landscape and relationships and skills and so on.

00:07:12.102 --> 00:07:32.282
But when the surgeon operates, it's only very small actions and very small part that he's paying attention to, and he has to pay attention to it. And this is what is a dilemma of consciousness, that you can see things very high resolution, but you have to consciously apply yourself to it.

00:07:33.036 --> 00:07:44.468
And Jung says when you're too long in a certain mode, of existing, you start to think that this is everything there is in reality.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:08:03.594
So let's say you're with your car and you're focusing on fixing your car, but you might not notice that people are passing you by that you could ask, or you are so focused on one part of the car, for example, um, the oil that you forget to check whether the battery is dead.

00:08:04.273 --> 00:08:24.685
So this is an effect of a phenomenon called functional fixedness that you start to think only in very, let's say, repeating patterns because you're used to it. So it's a very beaten path that you take that often works, but sometimes it does not. So you have to normally apply your consciousness somewhere else.

00:08:24.685 --> 00:08:47.488
So Another advantage of consciousness is that of reflection. To not only see and find the problem by applying conscious energy on it, but rather to reflect, okay, why did this happen? Did I not fill up the tank? Did I not load the battery?

00:08:48.295 --> 00:09:08.046
Did I have no air in the tires? So to go back mentally in the past and think about it. And learn from it, and thus integrating this hidden unconscious knowledge from the past into the present consciousness to prevent those errors from happening again.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:28.494
So that's roughly the understanding that Jung had from consciousness. And consciousness develops, as I already said in this small example of the car breaking down. It happens also in the lifetime of every human being.

00:09:29.408 --> 00:09:44.611
When a human being starts out as a baby, baby is just, and also a small child, pure instinct, just pure emotion. There's no really continuity. There's no reflecting. There's no willful action and so on.

00:09:45.285 --> 00:10:04.325
This bundle of energy is just acting how it feels like, or rather how it's acted upon. Something in the child is suddenly hungry, sleepy, angry, joyous, and so on. No continuity, no sustained willful action.

00:10:05.968 --> 00:10:36.054
And it is through education, through the parents, but also the family or society and school, university, or at work, that human beings able to develop a more robust consciousness, to make it grow by giving knowledge and training, but also to train consciousness in the sense of applying attention, applying will, and sustaining that will over time.

00:10:38.073 --> 00:10:51.321
And this is what happens in the beginning of a life till you reach adulthood and have a job. And normally in that case, you're already established.

00:10:54.194 --> 00:11:15.246
So this example of one's life can also be taken to whole of humanity, from an individual life to the life of humanity all in all. As humanity and humans are just like animals, but as far as we can see, most animals do not have consciousness.

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:32.791
They live on instincts and they're successful, but they are also very dependent on strict environmental parameters where they can act and where all those instincts make sense. Human beings can adapt.

00:11:33.424 --> 00:11:50.505
To changing environments, changing situations through consciousness, by discrimination, prioritization, willful acting, reflection, and so on. But this is all a fairly new— acquirement Jung saw.

00:11:51.228 --> 00:12:15.718
So there is a past where human beings acted more like animals, and at a certain point something very unique happened, and this is this acquirement of consciousness and this generation of willful acts. And as Jung sees the psyche, it's always building stuff on top of each other.

00:12:17.196 --> 00:12:42.195
Similar how the brain has certain areas that are incredibly old, so old that they're also called the reptile part of the brain because you also have it in reptiles. And other animals, and you have more sophisticated stuff around it. And Jung says deep in every human being, there is this, he calls primitive psychology.

00:12:43.190 --> 00:13:05.892
Primitive, not being a value judgment, but rather a description that can be best understood as a pre-scientific or we see it in a psychological manner. And he talks a lot about primitives in his work. So I will use this word also to give you an understanding what he meant by that.

00:13:06.981 --> 00:13:26.499
He saw himself also as a primitive, or rather being able to think in this primitive psychology because it's superstitious, it's pre-scientific, it's having ideas about the world and how it is. That are not verified in the sense where we would say, okay, this is how science is done.

00:13:30.051 --> 00:13:53.079
And this primitive psychology can still be observed according to Jung in hunter-gatherer tribes, indigenous people in South America and Africa, but also in Asia. But he said, It's not like modernity is something that is distributed evenly and everywhere the same.

00:13:54.012 --> 00:14:16.841
This means, he said, you can even find primitive people in the tram, but the same way there are people who are psychologically still in the Middle Ages. Or he said there are people who are psychologically in the year 3000, way ahead. There's always this differences in people that are more a matter of fact.

00:14:17.934 --> 00:14:38.401
Again, not a value judgment, it's just seeing how psychology works. And he was really interested in psychology of human beings. And as he saw this as a foundation of psychology, he investigated it and he went into Africa and South America.

00:14:39.092 --> 00:14:58.343
And also a lot of other places to directly talk with the people and to experience how they experience the world. Because a lot of these things are still present in the modern world. And even for us, he says, this layer of civilization is pretty thin. It's an acquirement which is very recent.

00:14:59.369 --> 00:15:20.586
Human beings as they are existing today anatomically is roughly 2 million years. But when you look how long culture exists, or even written recordings, that's just a couple of thousand years. So culture, maybe a little bit over 10,000 years, and writing, couple of thousand years.

00:15:20.634 --> 00:15:40.253
And this is also the point where Jung says their consciousness really is developed and started when people started to write things down, because it's a form of reflection. So what would he see in this primitive psychology, as he said?

00:15:42.330 --> 00:16:02.209
And he's said that it's recent to the point to the Roman times. For him, the Roman psychology was also a primitive psychology. And it's very strongly associated with unconscious and the way he saw the unconscious, same as a child is unconscious.

00:16:03.254 --> 00:16:26.719
And it's only those little islands, he called, that rise up from just some moments above the surface of the sea of the unconscious. And there reflection and willful action and so on can take place and then disappear again when it's exhausted, as consciousness exhausts itself. So we have to sleep every day, right?

00:16:26.783 --> 00:16:51.351
And if you apply it to your mind, willfully to something for a long time, you're just exhausted and you need to do something else and you need to relax and you have to recharge. So just as in a child, this is only very short amounts of time that get longer and longer with time where it can act consciously. The same is also true for primitive psychology, Jung says.

00:16:51.544 --> 00:17:14.369
So it's very strong, characterized by unconscious attributes. And unconscious for Jung means this mixed and entangled perception that things are not clearly differentiated and separated, as this is a function of consciousness to separate.

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:21.167
He saw a lack of separation between internal and external world.

00:17:22.119 --> 00:17:44.968
So when he saw primitives or indigenous people or hunter-gatherer tribes, as whatever you want to call it, talk about ghosts, he rather saw this as people experiencing their thoughts without realizing they're their own, or having dreams and thinking they're also real events happening.

00:17:45.753 --> 00:18:02.038
And This is what he's calling participation mystique. You cannot really separate yourself from the surroundings, or you deal in incorrect separation between the surroundings and yourself.

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:24.718
When you look at, for example, the Romans or the Greek gods, they very often symbolized emotions. And when you have ancient Greek literature, they are often described as emotions. So people were not really in love, but rather they were struck by the arrow of Eros.

00:18:25.793 --> 00:18:47.179
And they were not angry, but rather Ares drove into them. It's this externalization of emotions that are then characterized as human beings, as gods that are still human-like with superhuman powers. And immortal, but still human-like in their appearance.

00:18:48.046 --> 00:19:08.454
That characterizes a separation of things that are normally internal to see in the external, but also to be more connected to the environment. One example is this idea of rain dances or dances for good harvest.

00:19:10.603 --> 00:19:35.553
And it's really this idea that because people are doing something, it is happening in the outside world. Carl Gustav Jung talked about an encounter he had with the Pueblo Indians in South America. And every morning they would come out of the hut, greet the sun by spitting in their hands and showing the palms of their hands to the sky.

00:19:37.398 --> 00:20:01.249
And their idea was that they were causing the sun to rise. It was these actions that made the sun rise. And they got very angry when the white people showed up and the white people tried to discourage this behavior because they said, if they don't stop, there will be no more sun in the future because they're taking care for the sun to rise.

00:20:03.547 --> 00:20:27.145
And this is an amazing connection just to the external world and to the environment that today it's pretty much lacking. Today people are more atomized and said, okay, I have no influence over everything or anything. And this is a way that's still working in the psyche according to Jung. So.

00:20:27.713 --> 00:20:51.653
Anthropomorphizing the gods, the emotions. Participation mystique is this effect called of not being separated really from the outside world. Jung had also this example of when people could make bows, for example, they said they could do this because their grandfather was a bow. And this was a completely valid explanation.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:21:11.996
People could be coming from bows or boats or anything else. And he also said there were other people who said, okay, we are parrots. Except for our physical body, we are parrots, which is interesting.

00:21:16.084 --> 00:21:40.361
And this lack of consciousness or conscious differentiation So what we see also in the modern world is a strong degree of, of abstraction. This is an achievement of modernity and civilization to abstract things out, to have, for example, numbers that symbolize something.

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:45.414
They're not the thing, but there's something superordinate above all things.

00:21:46.945 --> 00:22:10.759
When many decades ago, they wanted to do— some researchers wanted to do IQ testing on indigenous people, and they had a pretty hard time to explain the concept of numbers to the people, because for a number to exist, you have to abstract and you have to say, yeah, all these things are the same.

00:22:11.819 --> 00:22:30.956
It was not that people could not count, but there was no need to count. For example, when there were people with bows and arrows, they would look at the arrows and with one glance you could see that everything is there because every arrow has a different characteristic and they know when one is missing.

00:22:31.871 --> 00:22:55.677
Same when you have shepherds, they know all those sheep because every sheep is different because in reality everything is a little bit different. So they don't need to count. They have the feeling whether everything is there. It's only the abstract mind that really said, okay, I'm just going to count. I'm going to reduce reality to a point where everything suddenly is the same.

00:22:56.930 --> 00:23:20.375
And this is of course an advantage of consciousness, but also a disadvantage because you're losing all those nuances and details of reality. And primitives or primitive psychology is way more detailed in that sense, as there's less abstraction.

00:23:20.613 --> 00:23:42.518
Carl Gustav Jung talked about how drawings of animals on walls or caves don't show the feet because the feet are mostly hidden in grass or mud or water or whatever. You really rarely see the feet of animals. So they were not depicting them because what they saw was not an animal with feet.

00:23:44.816 --> 00:24:03.435
And it's this extreme empiricism, let's say, by depicting things how they really are with all their details, that is different from our understanding, which is more abstract. You draw an animal, of course you draw feet because an animal has feet, even though that you rarely see them.

00:24:07.264 --> 00:24:29.458
He saw this lack of separation, lack of abstraction, as an advantage in the sense that people were way more aware of their psychology and things that were happening internally because they were also experiencing the things that happened internally outside. This phenomenon is called projection.

00:24:30.769 --> 00:24:47.108
You don't see the things necessarily how they are outside, but you see your psychological contents. But by being aware of this, they were closer to human nature and how psychology works.

00:24:47.253 --> 00:25:08.299
Something where Jung says the modern world is so noisy and consciousness is so strong that it pushes everything else away. Into a dark corner, into the unconscious where it stays. But there are dangers in the unconscious as it can disintegrate consciousness and overwhelm it.

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:22.067
And this is an explanation he had for a lot of different behavior and patterns of behavior that primitive people showed.

00:25:22.797 --> 00:25:47.306
This fear of losing oneself, of losing the soul, as he said, of losing consciousness and by being overwhelmed by instincts and emotions or ghosts, as the primitives would say. So they would envision all those safeguards against being overwhelmed.

00:25:47.532 --> 00:25:58.186
And Carl Gustav Jung was a great fearfulness of unusual things and of changes.

00:25:59.576 --> 00:26:17.104
And he noticed that primitives are very polite, very polite, as they not try to overwhelm the consciousness of the other party that all those unbundled emotions come out.

00:26:18.463 --> 00:26:38.217
Think of a child that is not like a small child, but it's a full-grown man that starts to have a temper tantrum, which can hurt the person or other persons also. So it's this fear of being overwhelmed by the unconscious, by impersonal factors.

00:26:39.685 --> 00:27:00.525
So what are all those safeguards that are available, because this is already a seed to understanding how consciousness developed more and more. He saw a lot of rituals centered around getting people into a specific mood.

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:14.541
So when we have to suspect that the general degree of consciousness is pretty low, and very fragile, applying of willful actions must be a problem.

00:27:15.577 --> 00:27:28.667
And he saw that when people were, for example, going on a hunt, they had to have a ritual for getting into the mind of a hunter and think like a hunter.

00:27:29.409 --> 00:27:51.602
So they would have dances and rituals to foster and bring rallying up people into this mood of acting like a hunter, to go out. Now is the time to hunt and be a hunter. He had this one situation where he wrote a letter.

00:27:51.667 --> 00:28:15.206
He was in Africa and he wanted to have it sent. So he gave it to one of the tribesmen and said, please go to the next city or town and give it to the post office so that it can be sent. And the person just looked at him and did not know what to do. But the guide, he was with him, with Jung, said, you're doing it completely wrong.

00:28:15.865 --> 00:28:40.278
He went to the guy and grabbed his arm with the letter in it and said, this is a letter. This is important. You will now run into the next city and you will not stop until you reach the city. And you will do everything to take this letter. You will go in there and say, I am this and this, and I have this letter, and I will send this now.

00:28:41.872 --> 00:29:01.073
And it was this whole song and dance that went on for minutes and minutes. But the other person finally got it and got into the mood, and then he ran off and He ran a crazy amount of time, like 40, 50, 60 kilometers without stopping because he was in that mindset and just kept him going.

00:29:02.051 --> 00:29:25.914
And this is still very similar to what we do today on concerts or political rallies or sport teams. This language of saying, we will do this, this now will be that. And bringing up the emotions and getting everyone in a state of mind. Motivational speakers do the same.

00:29:26.139 --> 00:29:44.281
It's motivation. And we still do all those things even when we are not aware of it. And this not being aware part of is also another thing that's characteristic of primitive psychology. Very often it's not really clear why something is done.

00:29:45.875 --> 00:30:10.407
And Jung criticized this about other people when they talked about primitive psychology, because they said, ah, the people are dumb, they don't know why they're doing things. The example that I had with the Pueblo Indians, it took him a really long time to find out because the people were confused when he asked them. When he asked them. And we have still the same thing today.

00:30:11.188 --> 00:30:30.504
Even for us, his example is, if you would go to England and ask the people, okay, do you have any rituals or superstitions? The people would say, no, no, everything is rational. We know everything and no need to investigate something. We are fully aware of what we're doing.

00:30:31.588 --> 00:30:55.624
But then every year in spring, people would go in their garden. And hang on little colored eggs into the bushes and everywhere. And when you then would go to the same person and ask, okay, what are you doing? Why are you doing this? They would say, what do you mean? We've always done this. And it doesn't mean nothing. We just hang it up. But of course it does not mean nothing.

00:30:55.768 --> 00:31:11.961
This is Easter. Same when you suddenly start chopping down a tree and put it up in your living room. At Christmas. There is a meaning, but it does not have to be consciously formulated for people to really follow it.

00:31:13.394 --> 00:31:35.047
And here Jung sees the past of humans as something unconscious, as this is just done without thinking and without reflection. This does not mean it does not work or is bad or so, but this is the beginning how people act. They first act unconsciously and then through reflection they can infer why they have done something.

00:31:35.095 --> 00:31:49.408
This is something that happens in therapy, that people try to understand their past by investigating it consciously again. So you have this rituals to get people into moods.

00:31:49.745 --> 00:32:14.478
You have the politeness, so not to rouse the emotions of the other person so that they can not get overwhelmed because consciousness is not strong enough to keep those emotions at bay. You start having contracts and laws to make behavior standardized, standardized and foreseeable. It's a fun thing.

00:32:15.430 --> 00:32:37.810
It does not matter where, but human beings just have this instinct and urge to create laws. Every society, every culture, every tribe, they all have laws. And this is to, again, have understandable behavior and foreseeable, predictable behavior.

00:32:39.669 --> 00:33:01.205
And there are a lot of taboos associated with laws. And here Jung saw already a sign how to cultivate consciousness by having a taboo, by not being able to do certain things or say certain things, you have to apply conscious effort because you're not doing things.

00:33:02.126 --> 00:33:08.372
And this is strengthening already consciousness. It's like a workout because you have to watch yourself.

00:33:10.026 --> 00:33:44.162
When I was younger and in school and we started to have English lessons, a very common problem for German students when they learn English is the sound of TH, the, because it's not common to the German language and some students start to develop a lisp that was not there before, but very often how it's explained to German students is to cultivate a lisp and I started to cultivate that without me realizing it.

00:33:44.388 --> 00:34:05.849
I was quite, unconscious about it. And also my parents did not realize it was someone else who pointed it out to us because for us it was common. We did not really think about it and we were not aware of it, but an outside person came to us and said, yeah, well, Marcus is lisping. And then it's first hard to believe, but then, yeah, you find out pretty quickly.

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:26.623
So when you go into training to de-train this lisp. It is really about paying attention, not only by you, but also by the people around you. So everybody's sitting there when you talk to check whether you're lisping or not. And this is same as a taboo.

00:34:27.365 --> 00:34:42.912
You cannot do things and you have to pay attention, otherwise you get punished for it. In the case of lisping, it was knocking on the table, which was pretty loud. And you also felt bad because you thought, ah, okay, I'm— was lisping again.

00:34:49.275 --> 00:35:10.493
So, and similar with the gods who were pretty close and personal, as you had different gods all for different emotions, the further civilization came, It became monotheistic and more removed from the person. It became more abstract.

00:35:11.639 --> 00:35:31.365
Same as when you look in the Bible and you look in the Old Testament, how God is presented. In the Garden of Eden, it's really just a person and he's approachable. But as the Old Testament goes on and on and on and on, God becomes more and more abstract.

00:35:31.913 --> 00:35:51.528
He is then a burning bush with Moses, or he's just a voice, or later it's just a feeling. So it's already this development of consciousness and this abstracting more and more and removing more from the, let's say, complex bodily world with all those details.

00:35:53.409 --> 00:36:12.843
And When this development of consciousness took place, the seat of thoughts also changed. So our modern understanding is thoughts are happening in the brain. And the brain, of course, is in the head. So everything happens in the head.

00:36:13.919 --> 00:36:34.110
But what Jung noticed when he talked about, talked with indigenous and primitive people, they often would say, no, thoughts are happening in the stomach because they would have the sensation. We still have the same sensations when you are fearful or have strong emotions.

00:36:34.206 --> 00:36:56.143
You find that your belly or stomach is acting up. Also, the heart, which followed after the idea of the stomach being the seat of thinking, then the heart came, as your heart also feels a certain way when you are doing certain things or experiencing certain things.

00:36:58.460 --> 00:37:21.578
And again, it is development of abstraction. The first one is very bodily. Jung said when you have hysterics, they very often have digestive problems. So it's not hard to understand why you would think, okay, The stomach could be the seat of certain ideas, even about morality.

00:37:22.045 --> 00:37:39.265
You feel bad when you do something wrong. It's really feeling, and it can be in your stomach or in your heart. So it's not so far-fetched to think that this is where all this is coming from. But now we say, of course, everything is in the brain. This is abstract.

00:37:40.594 --> 00:38:02.685
The brain does not even have pain receptors, is why you can do brain surgery without any anesthesia. But of course you can feel a lot in your stomach and in your heart. And as the separation also takes place, you suddenly start to have more individuality.

00:38:05.474 --> 00:38:30.319
In a tribe, all the people are very much the same and everybody is connected. And this degree of individuality that is like basic for us today to say everybody is different, is their own person and so on. This is also fairly new discovery and accomplishment. Again, it's this idea of separation.

00:38:32.821 --> 00:38:51.182
So this brings us, of course, to the modern world where we now have a strong grasp on consciousness. At least we think that way. We reap all the benefits of consciousness, but also all the downsides of consciousness.

00:38:51.941 --> 00:39:12.057
And it's this discriminated, separated view that is hurting us in a certain regard. Carl Gustav Jung said humanity dreamt for the whole time about flying, being able to fly, soar through the air. And the first thing that people built were bombers.

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:35.914
So it's this cold rationality that makes a lot of things possible, a lot of negative things also. And with it comes a lack of morality as you again focused so much on something and how to do it and not really on the question, should you do it?

00:39:38.290 --> 00:40:04.828
And through these abstractions, we reached a very removed frame of mind and By separating the inner and the outer and having clearly more input from outside than from inside, there's this whole exaggeration of the importance of the outside world, which is in itself, it's dead again.

00:40:04.972 --> 00:40:24.420
This is materialism, which came up in the 19th century and also 20th century, where you treat the whole world basically like Minecraft. It's just. Blocks of material that you can use to do other stuff, but it's all dead outside.

00:40:26.086 --> 00:40:47.928
And this is a completely different view than primitive psychology where you say, this are the stones of ancients, or this is a holy lake and all those things. This is contaminated, of course, with internal psychology. But there's a connection to the outside, which is now more missing. And you have this hardcore materialism.

00:40:50.660 --> 00:41:03.357
A little bit like Freud also thought that everything comes from the outside because everything outside is dead, but it's very overwhelming as there's a lot of to explore outside.

00:41:04.227 --> 00:41:21.806
And it's this especially Western mind of ignoring the inner, where Jung saw his main mission to reintroduce psychology and the experiences of the inner to the Western mind, as this has been discounted.

00:41:23.990 --> 00:41:43.180
And even though that they were thinking, we're very powerful as we are very conscious and rational and scientific, and we can look at all those accomplishments, everything we have today is a result of conscious endeavor and improving things also.

00:41:45.368 --> 00:42:05.948
We are missing out on a lot of things, especially as this connection to nature, as Jung says, we're removed, we're like a bird that's rising, rising, rising, rising, and forgetting that it has a body. And as Again, consciousness is focused on a very small part and thinking, okay, it's all-powerful, it can do everything.

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:29.251
It's not noticing the things that are happening in the background and how easily it can break again. He really saw that in the atrocities of the First World War and Second World War. Jung lived through both wars and he served as a doctor. On the front lines of the First World War.

00:42:30.214 --> 00:42:52.457
And it was before that war, a hundred-year peace in Europe, more or less. There were smaller conflicts, but they were resolved rather quickly. It was after Napoleon, the idea was that war basically is over. And then there comes the absolute horror of the First World War.

00:42:53.068 --> 00:42:57.127
And then of course all the atrocities of the Second World War.

00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:26.221
And there he thought the main problem of the modern world, which is not lack of stuff or depression, but rather psychic epidemics that people get overwhelmed by emotions and especially ideas that are contagious and just gather large groups and cause tremendous damage.

00:43:26.850 --> 00:43:43.340
He saw this as a danger of ideology and a danger of all the isms, communism, fascism, that sprang up fairly recently. Even the idea of nationalism is not that old.

00:43:46.254 --> 00:43:55.107
And it was in his eyes, the breakdown of religion that caused this.

00:43:56.433 --> 00:44:17.817
As he said, again, just like laws or rituals or taboos, as they get codified in a religion, and this religion represents the inner experiences of the people, it can act as a psychotherapeutic way to help people. To navigate this dangers of the unconscious.

00:44:18.624 --> 00:44:40.139
But with time, this broke down and it has been going on for a very long time, for hundreds of years. It's a slow and steady process in his eyes. This left people pretty much unprepared for the things that can happen in the soul and to get swept again by unconscious forces.

00:44:40.916 --> 00:45:03.640
And this is where he thought lies the reason why psychology came up suddenly, as people were now confronted with their psychology. Before it was been taken care of, there have been all those institutions and rituals and taboos in place to prevent things from happening.

00:45:04.317 --> 00:45:24.285
And again, this was not a conscious decision. Decision from people in the past, but rather best practice, a Darwinism of best practices. And by disintegrating all those stuff, suddenly the floodgates were open again and all this has to be rediscovered.

00:45:25.012 --> 00:45:36.822
Of course, we cannot say just read the Bible, follow the Bible, and everything will be fine. The need for consciousness and the need for understanding is too high, he says.

00:45:37.576 --> 00:45:54.649
But culture and societies start to generate new germs and seeds of bringing this together, consciousness and the unconscious, and to have both harmonize together.

00:45:55.290 --> 00:46:18.392
And there he really saw psychology and the work of psychology and why Suddenly, 100 years ago, everybody became interested in it because people were suddenly confronted with their own psychology. It was not taken care of and they were alone with these problems. And this is what he would do with his patients when they would have problems.

00:46:19.462 --> 00:46:35.657
He would try to explain psychology to them only so far that they needed. He, saw himself really as a doctor. His job was to bring people back into their normal life. They were living a normal life.

00:46:36.283 --> 00:46:53.999
They had an experience that disrupted this, and he wanted to bring them back to have a job, have relationships, be a productive member of society. Because this is where a lot of people find meaning.

00:46:55.461 --> 00:47:15.654
And so he would give people enough knowledge so that they can understand this. And this is very often what I've heard from people when they, for example, listen to the biblical lectures of Jordan Peterson. He frames all this in psychological ways.

00:47:16.476 --> 00:47:39.359
And for many people, they are now interested in those Christian stories and teachings. After viewing them through a psychological lens. And maybe this is where Jung is right and says, yeah, this is the lens that we have to use today to see it through a psychological lens and have this language.

00:47:39.808 --> 00:47:56.882
He says the concepts and everything that he's talking about is very similar to that what existed hundreds, thousands of years ago. But the words are different. The connections are a little bit different. It's more living.

00:47:56.995 --> 00:48:15.529
It's not dead like religion is mostly for us, especially in the Western world. So, so this was my presentation in regards to A History of Consciousness.

00:48:18.124 --> 00:48:31.970
Through all the different texts and ideas that Jung has written. And I hope this was entertaining and that you learned a bit and that it helps to understand Jung better.

